"""Own complete original-field readings, authored before candidate or peer-note access."""
NOTES = {
('abudawud:2548','hi'): "Sahl ibn Hanzaliyah reports the Messenger passing a camel whose belly has become joined to its back, followed by the actual fear-Allah, proper-riding and proper-eating instruction concerning speechless beasts. The physical condition, object and both commands are supplied. Keep this complete short report and its own reporter identity without adding animal-care explanations.",
('abudawud:2548','ur'): "The complete Urdu account independently supplies Sahl, the emaciated camel and the Messenger's two practical instructions. Preserve the speechless-beasts restriction and the exact riding/eating predicates. The terminal numbered footnote marker is apparatus, not speech. No other version is needed to complete the text.",
('abudawud:2552','hi'): "Abu Bashir's own journey account supplies the resting-place setting, dispatched messenger and instruction to remove the camels' bowstring or other neck strings. Malik's appended belief that people used them against the evil eye is explanatory conjecture, not another reported action or Prophetic command. Preserve its defective printed wording as context, without repairing Hindi from Urdu.",
('abudawud:2552','ur'): "Read the actual travel/resting-place scene and the removal instruction in full. The later explicit Malik conjecture about the evil eye explains the command and belongs in source context. Keep both alternatives in the instruction and the exact source description of the strings; do not turn Malik's causal thought into the Messenger's words.",
('abudawud:2558','hi'): "The complete Abdullah ibn Umar report supplies the Messenger's prohibition of riding the dung-eating camels. The object and restriction are actual wording. Preserve the source's printed name spelling and complete short body without expanding the rule.",
('abudawud:2558','ur'): "The own Urdu field contains the same independently supplied prohibition with its dung-eating-camel restriction. It is a finite attributed report, with no appended compiler passage to admit or supply. Retain every source word.",
('abudawud:2562','hi'): "Ibn Abbas reports the Messenger's prohibition of making animals fight. This is complete finite wording rather than a bare heading or pointer. Keep its own attribution and object exactly, without adding an animal list or an authenticity claim.",
('abudawud:2562','ur'): "The short Urdu prohibition and Ibn Abbas attribution are complete. Keep that finite report and move only the terminal footnote apparatus to context. Do not infer any additional fight, animal or occasion.",
('abudawud:2563','hi'): "Anas brings his newborn brother for tahnik; the source then supplies an abbreviated barn/branding observation, with a damaged or absent observation-subject link. Preserve this own gap rather than borrowing Urdu's clearer link. Hisham's appended 'I think Anas said' contains an actual finite ear-branding variant: retain it separately under its expressly uncertain transmission, in the same account family. The ghutti and branding synonyms are lexical context.",
('abudawud:2563','ur'): "The complete Urdu account supplies bringing the newborn brother, the tahnik purpose and the observation of the Prophet branding sheep. Its Hisham-to-Anas ear-location variant is finite but explicitly uncertain, so retain it as a separate transmitted variant in the same family, not as Hisham's eyewitness account. The ghutti/branding synonyms and numbered footnote are context; the actual ear location remains.",
('abudawud:2578','hi'): "Aisha's own two-race account includes the first victory, later heavier body, second contest and the Messenger's matching-win response. Preserve that sequence, physical circumstance and brief dialogue exactly. No explanatory parenthesis or additional biography is required.",
('abudawud:2578','ur'): "The Urdu account independently supplies both races and the final balancing response. The printed 'again' parenthesis qualifies the repetition of the actual race and remains narrative scope, rather than a detachable glossary. Preserve the weight/time relation and every source word.",
('abudawud:2582','hi'): "Qatadah's named sentence explains that the terms Jalab and Janab concern horse racing. It is a lexical/domain explanation without an independent reported event or substantive saying beyond defining those terms. Read the remaining languages before any whole-parent source-context decision; numbering and the named formula alone do not establish matn.",
('abudawud:2582','ur'): "The complete Urdu field likewise defines the domain of Jalab and Janab, followed by a numbered note. It supplies no distinct narrated occasion or further body. This is provisionally explanation context, pending the complete sibling-language check rather than automatic rejection from the Hindi field.",
('abudawud:2582','ar'): "The complete Arabic chain ends with Qatadah's al-jalab/al-janab fi al-rihan definition. No additional finite event or report follows. Preserve the entire chain and definition as source context; this own field does not establish another body to admit.",
('abudawud:2582','en'): "The complete English expands the two terms as taking another horse behind or alongside a horse in a race. Its damaged closing punctuation is preserved. This is the same term definition, with no separate event; the expanded lexical paraphrase is not converted into a new report.",
('abudawud:2582','ur-Latn'): "The complete Roman field names Qatada and says the two terms occur in horse-racing contests. It remains a term-domain explanation despite its honorific and transmission formula. All five own fields have now been read and no independently report-bearing body was found.",
('abudawud:2583','hi'): "Anas supplies an actual description of the Messenger's sword handle/sheath as printed, including the defective Hindi material spelling. A physical possession/practice report is eligible without literal quoted speech. Keep the defect and do not substitute Urdu's cleaner silver word.",
('abudawud:2583','ur'): "Anas's actual sword-fitting material description is complete in this own field. Preserve the printed silver description and object without adding dimensions or reconciling the Hindi spelling. No source-critical appendix is present.",
('abudawud:2592','hi'): "Jabir reports the Messenger's white flag on the day he entered Makkah. This is an actual historical observation; retain the place, day-relative setting and colour exactly, without supplying a calendar date or an externally inferred campaign identity.",
('abudawud:2592','ur'): "The complete Urdu field independently supplies Jabir, the Makkah entry and white banner. Preserve the actual bounded historical description and do not expand the event from another source.",
('abudawud:2610','hi'): "Nafi transmits Abdullah ibn Umar's prohibition of travelling with the Quran into enemy territory. The Hindi then has a damaged repeated causal tail ('man kiya ki' and defective final verb), without naming a reasoning speaker or marking a quoted report. Keep the clear prohibition; retain the ambiguous explanatory tail as context rather than supplying Malik's identity from the other languages or silently repairing the broken link. The initial short Nafi attribution may be separated as transmission context while preserving the final reporter's own predicate.",
('abudawud:2610','ur'): "The Urdu prohibition is followed by an explicitly attributed Malik conjecture: he thinks the reason is that the enemy could seize it. The latter is explanation context. Preserve the actual Ibn Umar/Prophetic prohibition and its object/domain, separating Nafi's prefatory transmission link without stripping the final reporter predicate. No causal claim is relabelled as Prophetic speech.",
('abudawud:2610','ar'): "The complete Arabic has a multi-step route through al-Qanabi, Malik and Nafi, then Ibn Umar's actual prohibition. Its explicit Malik arahu makhafata clause is conjectural explanation. Separate the full prefatory route and explanatory tail; preserve the coherent final-reporter predicate and all actual prohibition wording. No missing link is manufactured.",
('abudawud:2610','en'): "The English independently states Ibn Umar's prohibition and then identifies the narrator Malik's 'I think' fear-of-seizure explanation. Admit the finite prohibition only, keeping the latter context with its original broken closing punctuation. This own field supports the boundary without transferring any wording into Hindi.",
('abudawud:2610','ur-Latn'): "The complete Roman field explicitly distinguishes Nafi's route, Ibn Umar's prohibition and Malik's personal causal thought. Preserve the own final-reporter prohibition, keep the prefatory link and conjecture as context, and do not use the Roman text to repair the damaged Hindi tail.",
('abudawud:2617','hi'): "The named hearing frame introduces an Ubna/Yubna-in-Palestine name identification. The source's defective Abu-Mushir/Ubna spellings remain exact. This appears to be geographical correction apparatus rather than an independent substantive historical account; all other fields must be read before a whole-parent decision.",
('abudawud:2617','ur'): "The complete Urdu mentions the place name before Abu Mushir and quotes his identification of it as Yubna in Palestine. The hearing formula does not add an independent event body beyond the geographic gloss. Preserve the exact names and qualification; assess all three remaining fields rather than borrowing this spelling into Hindi.",
('abudawud:2617','ar'): "The complete Arabic route and hearing statement end in the correction from Ubna to Yubna of Palestine. There is no additional event or independently transmitted substantive body after that place-name explanation. Preserve the entire source as geographic/transmission context.",
('abudawud:2617','en'): "The complete English says Abu Mishar was told about Ubna and replied that they know it as Yubna of Palestine. It is a place-name identification, with the printed incomplete punctuation retained. It does not supply a separate historical narration beyond explaining the source locator.",
('abudawud:2617','ur-Latn'): "The complete Roman hearing frame and Yubna/Palestine identification confirm that this own field contains the same geographical explanation. All five fields have now been fully read. Preserve the whole numbered source as context instead of promoting the definition because it has a named speaker.",
('abudawud:2618','hi'): "Anas supplies the Messenger's actual dispatch of Basisa as a spy to inspect Abu Sufyan's caravan. Preserve the named mission, purpose and source spelling exactly; do not correct the envoy's name from outside parallels or infer a larger battle account.",
('abudawud:2618','ur'): "The complete Urdu dispatch/spy/caravan report is finite and independently supplied. Retain the exact source names and mission purpose without adding an unprinted outcome, date or battle.",
('tirmidhi:2549','hi'): "The complete Hindi field is a compact source-rendered account of the Paradise market, Friday visit, platforms, divine conversation/forgiveness, unbought gifts, meetings and return to spouses. Preserve it as this source's own condensed transmitted account; do not expand it using Urdu's much longer dialogue. The short Said-to-Abu-Hurayrah route can remain context before the final reporter predicate. The embedded divine speech/actions are reported within the account, not separately asserted external facts.",
('tirmidhi:2549','ur'): "The complete long Urdu field begins with Said's encounter with Abu Hurayrah, the latter's wish to meet in the market, Said's question and Abu Hurayrah's report from the Messenger. Keep that actual reception dialogue separately attributed from the transmitted Paradise account, without treating every nested divine/inhabitant quotation as a new occurrence. The account's no-truly-inferior-rank parenthesis is a substantive limitation and remains. The repeated lower-rank-object label and 'we (Paradise people)' identification are explanatory aliases. Tirmidhi's numbered grade and other-route notice are compiler context. Preserve every conditional, question, rank qualification, scent, garment change and return dialogue.",
('tirmidhi:2551','hi'): "Jarir reports the seated moon observation, future sight, absence of crowding and instruction around sunrise/sunset. Preserve the source's own after-setting timing even though the subsequent verse says before setting; do not correct it from canonical knowledge. The Fajr/Maghrib parenthesis is a prayer-name explanation of the already printed times. The actually recited verse and its complete parenthetic translation remain within the report; only the appended grading is compiler context.",
('tirmidhi:2551','ur'): "The Urdu field independently supplies Jarir's scene, seeing comparison, crowding qualification, prayer instruction and the actual verse with translation. Preserve its own time wording and prayer-name gloss without silently replacing Maghrib with another prayer. Keep the verse and translation as recitation content, while the prayer-name gloss, numbered footnote and final grading remain context.",
('tirmidhi:2558','hi'): "Abu Said's reported account supplies death as a mottled ram, its position between Paradise and Hell, slaughter before their inhabitants and the two joy/grief counterfactuals. Keep all finite clauses and the contrasting counterfactual limits. Tirmidhi's appended grading is context rather than part of the report.",
('tirmidhi:2558','ur'): "Read the complete independent Urdu ram/slaughter/observers account and both counterfactual conclusions. Preserve their scope and source wording; retain the honorific parenthesis, but exclude the final Tirmidhi grading from narration. No familiar longer version is inserted.",
('tirmidhi:2565','hi'): "Yahya's verse interpretation and definition of listening are explanatory material. Within that explanation, however, the source explicitly prints a finite hadith fragment that the houris raise their song voices. That actual bounded fragment can be retained under an unknown ultimate speaker, with its supplied 'what came in the hadith' frame; do not relabel Yahya's exegesis or the Quran verse as Prophetic narration. Read the other fields to inspect their own clause, not to infer an absent complete hadith.",
('tirmidhi:2565','ur'): "The complete Urdu likewise contains verse/exegesis followed by an explicitly reported finite hadith clause about the houris raising their singing voices. Keep that own bounded clause and its report frame with the ultimate speaker/chain unknown; the meaning-of-listening discussion remains context. Do not invent which other hadith is being referenced or turn the explanation into a new canonical saying.",
('tirmidhi:2565','ar'): "The full Arabic supplies a long route to Yahya, a verse and listening gloss, then the actual finite quoted report clause introduced as what came in the hadith: al-hur al-in raise their voices. The route/exegesis is context. The explicitly supplied fragment is independently present in this field and may be admitted with its unknown ultimate attribution and abbreviated scope, without completing the earlier report.",
('tirmidhi:2565','en'): "The complete English has Yahya's verse gloss and an actual clause introduced by 'what has been mentioned in the Hadith' concerning Al-Hur Al-Ein raising their voices. Preserve that finite own fragment and its unknown attribution; do not treat the compiler's meaning-of-listening explanation or the quoted verse as the hadith body. The absent final punctuation is unchanged.",
('tirmidhi:2565','ur-Latn'): "The complete Roman field independently prints the verse/listening explanation and the finite report that the Hoor-een raise their song voices. Keep only the latter with its explicit hadith-reference frame, qualified as abbreviated and ultimately unattributed. Preserve the source spelling and do not create the missing parent narration.",
('tirmidhi:2579','hi'): "Abu Hurayrah transmits the brief statement that the unbeliever's molar will be like Uhud. The comparison and actual attribution are complete. Keep the finite short report; Tirmidhi's hasan label is compiler context and not an authenticity determination by this review.",
('tirmidhi:2579','ur'): "The complete Urdu supplies the same independently stated molar/Uhud comparison before Tirmidhi's assessment. Preserve the actual short body and source speaker exactly, and leave the assessment in context without adding anatomical dimensions or another version.",
('tirmidhi:2619','hi'): "The opening points to A'mash and a preceding chain, but the field also actually supplies the finite contrast between a servant and shirk or kufr through abandoning prayer. Separate the prefatory route/pointer, retaining the 'in which it was said' report predicate and actual clause. The ultimate utterer is not named in this field and must remain unknown rather than being filled from another occurrence.",
('tirmidhi:2619','ur'): "The Urdu independently prints the previous-chain pointer and a finite statement about leaving prayer. Keep the bounded supplied wording with its own anonymous report predicate, while the A'mash route/pointer stays context. Do not infer a Companion, the Prophet, or a complete preceding hadith from the shared number or familiar wording.",
}
